# Ambar's own tools

- **Scope is the core rule.** `crates/ambar-recon/src/scope.rs` is the only scope implementation; Workbench calls `ambar-recon scope`. Every command that sends packets checks each target (and resolved addresses) first and fails closed. Without a scope file only loopback is allowed. Keep the engagement dates and the activity log.
- These tools find, check, organise and report. Don't add exploitation, payloads, credential attacks or anything that changes a remote system; the Kali tools installed by `ambar-sec` cover that under the user's own control.
- `ambar-recon`: Rust, tokio, rustls (aws-lc-rs provider, same as reqwest). JSON lines with `--json`. Integration tests in `tests/recon.rs` run the real binary against local servers; keep it that way.
- `ambar-engage`: findings importers exist for ambar-recon, nmap XML and nuclei JSONL; add importers with a test fixture. Severities: critical, high, medium, low, info.
- `ambar-guard`: checks only read; each fix is a list of commands plus a sentence describing it. Read the system under `AMBAR_GUARD_ROOT` so tests can use a fake root; connections come from `/proc/net/*` and `/proc/*/fd`.
- `ambar-clean`: only known, rebuildable locations; never follow symlinks; the Trash needs explicit confirmation.
- `ambar-share`: 128-bit random link, only the named files, expiry, constant-time token compare.
- Apps are thin PySide6 windows over the CLI; app tests run offscreen in their own process.
